Z||000021||88formula||10-13-2001||07:48 PM||||The fiero was never meant to be a straight-line drag car like the Mustang is. The fiero is a very well handling mid engine sports car. It’s in a totally different class and they are like comparing apples to oranges. You may be able to make a mustang faster than a fiero for less money but I bet even a stock fiero can take a modified mustang on a road course with the right drivers. It’s also very easy to make a fiero pull over 1G in lateral acceleration on a road course.  Besides you are comparing an 80’s car with a late 90’s car. When you think of it that way the fiero actually does pretty well. Imagine what would have come of the fiero if it were still in production? There was talk of giving it an aluminum frame and bigger more powerful engines.  <P>I’m not one of those people who likes to takes sides. I like all sports cars especially foreign sports cars. I think you guys are just wasting your breath and you will never convince each other of your views on what is the better car. That’s all in one persons opinion anyway!  I like Mustangs and I like the sound that 5.0-liter engines make with a Flowmaster exhaust. My friend has one! I love the sound of the v6 fiero too. If the Mustang was not such a great sports car itself than why would it be in production for so long? If the guy says his 150 horsepower mustang beat a v6 fiero than so what. That doesn’t mean all v6 Mustangs can beat all v6 fieros in a race, and if so who cares? That v6 fiero he raced probably had some problems too or was not running right or maybe the fiero guy can’t drive. I say this because a 2001 190 horsepower Mustang on paper is just as fast as an 85 v6 fiero was new and therefore a v6 fiero should be faster than a 95 150 horsepower Mustang.   <P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
