Z||000028||Formula||10-14-2001||01:41 AM||||Dan,<BR>I think the stealth is in a league with the cobra-R and the nova is up there with something like a stealth fighter.<P>My fiero stock (5hp at the wheels) never had to many problems with 2000 V6 5-spd stangs. We ran side by side all the way down the road (auto vs. stick too!).   I think from my brief experience driving Dan's GT that it would have toasted a V6 stang.  But, when my old engine was on its lasts legs it was getting toasted by the old 2.3 L stangs heh.<P>Hopefully after this week it will be a different ballgame!  I HOPE I can run a mid 14 with the new motor.||||reg||1
Z||000029||kslish||10-14-2001||02:36 AM||||I rented a 2000 V6 w/auto Mustang a little over a month ago for a weekend trip to N. Carolina.  It had around 15,000 miles on it.  Nice car, but not a real exciting performer.  I'd say 0-60 in roughly 8.5-9.0 seconds with the auto.  Mustangs are pretty heavy cars for their size.  The engine has ample torque, but isn't real happy in the upper RPMs (redline was 5500 if I recall correctly).  Decent highway cruiser though with good fuel economy.  Any Fiero V6 in decent shape should be able to have an easy time versus a Mustang V6 auto.  The stick shift Mustang V6 may be a little better competition.  This is just versus the newer Mustang V6's though...after experiencing the Mustang 190hp V6 I think the older 150hp V6 Mustangs would probably be real dogs.  <P>My biggest gripe about the car was the muted exhaust note of the V6 (especially in comparison to say a V6 Fiero).  It wouldn't sound aggressive reguardless of how hard it was pushed.  Fine for say a Taurus, but not a Mustang.<P>I have friends that have Mustang Cobras and so on though, so I know how fast these cars can be...but a Mustang V6 is only an adequate "economy" performer at best.<P>Ken S.||||reg||1
